      "narration": "Two numbers get a packet to the right place. The IP address identifies the machine, and the port identifies which program on that machine should receive it. That is the whole model, and holding it clearly explains most of what follows. Some addresses are worth recognizing on sight. One-two-seven dot zero dot zero dot one is localhost, meaning this machine and only this machine — traffic to it never leaves the box. The ten-dot, one-seventy-two-dot-sixteen-through-thirty-one, and one-ninety-two-dot-one-sixty-eight ranges are private, used inside home networks and cloud VPCs, and not routable on the public internet. And zero dot zero dot zero dot zero is not really an address at all; when a server binds to it, it means listen on every interface I have. Which brings us to the single most common networking confusion in deployment, so let us name it now. If a service binds to one-two-seven dot zero dot zero dot one, it will work perfectly when you test it from the same machine and refuse every connection from anywhere else. Works locally but refused remotely is almost always this. The fix is binding to zero dot zero dot zero dot zero — and then, because you have just exposed it to the world, making sure your firewall is doing its job.",
